E=[
(2,1875,"Mysteries","An arm pierced as by a musket ball","A man trundling a cart from Schaffhausen near Beringen, Germany has his right arm perforated from front to back as if by a musket ball. Popular Science reported it, and Fort found that no shooter could be traced.","NL","New Lands, citing Popular Science, 15-566","a man was trundling a cart from Schaffhausen, near Beringen, Germany"),
(3,1843,"Haunts","Three white figures over Warwick","At Warwick, Canada West, Charles Cooper hears a rumbling in the sky and sees a cloud under which three human forms, 'perfectly white', sail through the air above him, not higher than the treetops.","NL","New Lands, on Charles Cooper of Warwick, C.W.","somebody named Charles Cooper heard a rumbling sound in the sky"),
(4,1844,"Sky","Glaisher's signal light","Glaisher reports a light like a signal light, as bright as Jupiter, 'sending out quick flickering waves of light'. Fort cites the Year Book of Facts.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Year Book of Facts, 1845-278","something like a signal light, reported by Glaisher, Oct. 4, 1844"),
(5,1915,"Falls","A month of fall over Australia","Starting on Oct. 5 an enormous fall of a fine substance covers thousands of square miles of Australia, until the 29th. The Scientific Australian described it a year later.","NL","New Lands, citing Scientific Australian, Sept. 1916","from October 5 to 29, 1915, an enormous fall of similar substance occurred"),
(6,1863,"Mysteries","A great explosion that was a quake","A disturbance now listed as an earthquake is reported in so many London papers as a great explosion that Fort asks why. A similar concussion followed in the same region on Oct. 30, 1868.","NL","New Lands, citing London newspapers","Upon Oct. 6, 1863, there was a disturbance that is now listed as an earthquake"),
(7,1841,"Falls","Fishes fall at Dunfermline","Many fishes several inches long fall at Dunfermline in a thunderstorm, three months after another fish-fall that July. The Times printed the report five days later.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, Oct. 12, 1841","at Dunfermline, three months later (Oct. 7, 1841) fell many fishes"),
(8,1844,"Falls","Jelly beside a falling light at Coblenz","At night near Coblenz, a German acquaintance of Greg and another person see a luminous body fall close to them. Next morning they find a gelatinous mass of grayish colour. Greg wrote to Prof. Baden-Powell.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rept. Brit. Assoc., 1855-94","at night, Oct. 8, 1844, near Coblenz, a German, who was known to Greg"),
(9,1907,"Falls","Black rain in Ireland","A black rain falls in Ireland on Oct. 8 and 9. It leaves 'a most peculiar and disagreeable smell in the air'. Nature explained it as a cloud of soot from South Wales.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Symons' Met. Mag., 43-2; Nature, March 2, 1908","A black rain fell in Ireland, Oct. 8 and 9, 1907"),
(10,1802,"Cosmos","Fritsche's unknown body","Fritsche sees an unknown body of planetary size cross the Sun. It is the earliest of the six observations Le Verrier formulated as evidence of an intra-Mercurial planet.","BD","Book of the Damned, on Le Verrier's six observations","Fritsche, Oct. 10, 1802"),
(11,1847,"Cosmos","Schmidt's doubtful observation","Schmidt observes an unknown body near the Sun. Fort notes the observation was called doubtful until another page of the record revealed the doubt came from a mistranslation.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Notices of the R.A.S., 20-100","An observation by Schmidt, Oct. 11, 1847, is said to be doubtful"),
(12,1839,"Sky","A sky hung with sackcloth at Comrie","At the time of a quake at Comrie, the Rev. M. Walker says the sky was 'peculiarly strange and alarming' and looked as if hung with sackcloth. Fort adds it to the Comrie sounds and sky effects.","NL","New Lands, citing Mallet's catalogue","appeared as if hung with sackcloth"),
(13,1872,"Falls","The stone that hung in the sky of Servia","A stone said to be of an unknown kind falls near Soko-Banja, Servia. Fort dates it 1872 and says its strangeness is the point of the datum.","NL","New Lands (Fort's date; not independently checked)","Upon October 13, 1872, a stone fell from the sky, to this earth, near the town of Soko-Banja, Servia"),
(14,1877,"Sky","A huge ball of green fire","In a gale something falls that is described as a 'huge ball of green fire'. Several correspondents write about it to Nature and the Times.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, Oct. 25, 1877 and London Times, Oct. 15, 1877","fell in a gale of Oct. 14, 1877"),
(15,1755,"Mysteries","Meteorites over Lisbon","Fort lists 'numerous meteorites' at Lisbon in Mallet's catalogue of earthquakes accompanied by strange things in the sky, a few days before the great quake of Nov. 1.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Mallet's catalogue","numerous meteorites, Lisbon, Oct. 15, 1755"),
(16,1883,"Falls","Woolly lumps at Montussan","A thick cloud with rain and violent wind appears at Montussan in the Gironde, composed of a woolly substance in lumps the size of a fist. A correspondent sent a sample to La Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing La Nature, 1883-342","said to have fallen at Montussan (Gironde), Oct. 16, 1883"),
(17,1870,"Sky","Shadows and luminous images at Bangalore","Lieut. Herschel, observing the Sun at Bangalore, sees dark shadows crossing it, and away from the Sun, luminous moving images. For two days bodies pass. Fort cites Monthly Notices.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Notices, 30-135","Oct. 17 and 18, 1870, while observing the sun, at Bangalore, India"),
(18,1737,"Falls","A rain of earth at Carpentras","A rain of earth falls at Carpentras, France. Fort lists it with other strange events preceding earthquakes in Mallet's catalogue.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Mallet's catalogue","rain of earth, Carpentras, France, Oct. 18, 1737"),
(20,1824,"Cosmos","A light flashes on the dark Moon","At five in the morning Gruithuisen sees a light on the dark part of the Moon. It disappears, reappears after six minutes and then flashes intermittently until sunrise at 5:30.","NL","New Lands, citing Gruithuisen","morning of Oct. 20, 1824, a light was seen upon the dark part of the moon"),
(23,1839,"Sky","Black particles over Comrie","At Comrie, sounds seem to come from the sky and clothes bleaching on the grass are covered with black particles that presumably fell. Milne reported it among the 247 occurrences there.","NL","New Lands, citing David Milne, Edin. New Phil. Jour.","upon Oct. 23, 1839, something had occurred in the sky"),
(24,1864,"Cosmos","Red lights on Mars","At Mars' nearest approach, red lights are seen on opposite parts of the planet. Fort cites Comptes Rendus and asks whether signals might have been exchanged with the Moon.","NL","New Lands, citing Comptes Rendus, 85-538","Oct. 24, 1864-period of nearest approach by Mars-red lights upon opposite parts of Mars"),
(25,1889,"Mysteries","Sheep stampede across thirty square miles","The Rev. J. Ross Barker of Chesham writes that many flocks of sheep, across a region of 30 square miles, broke from their folds by common impulse. He asks whether anyone knew of a meteor or earthquake.","NL","New Lands, citing London Standard, Nov. 7, 1889","upon Oct. 25, 1889, many flocks of sheep, in a region of 30 square miles"),
(27,1890,"Sky","Eddie's comet that moved 100 degrees","At Grahamstown, Eddie observes a comet-like body that moved 100 degrees while visible. Fort cites it against astronomers who held that objects like it were not seen. Dr. A. G. Bell saw it later.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 43-89, 90","a comet-like body, of Oct. 27, 1890, observed at Grahamstown, by Eddie"),
(28,1812,"Haunts","Phantom soldiers at Havarah Park","Phantom soldiers are seen at Havarah Park near Ripley, England. Fort cites the Edinburgh Annual Register, and notes meteorologists call such sights displays of the aurora.","NL","New Lands, citing Edinburgh Annual Register, 1812-II-124","at Havarah Park, near Ripley, England"),
(30,1868,"Mysteries","Another concussion over Hereford","A concussion occurs in about the same region as that of Oct. 6, 1863. It is widely attributed to a great explosion, perhaps in London, and then supposed an earthquake when no terrestrial explosion could be found.","NL","New Lands","There was a similar concussion in about the same region, Oct. 30, 1868"),
]
